        <div id="three-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Three Kinds of Garage Floor Mat
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Containment mats</strong> are flexible PVC sheets with a raised perimeter, usually a foam or rolled edge around an inch high. They are built for one job: holding the snow, slush and salt brine that drips off a car after a winter drive. Without one, that water spreads across the floor, carries road salt into every crack and expansion joint, and refreezes into ice near the door. With one, it sits in a tray until it evaporates or you push it out with a squeegee.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Roll-out vinyl flooring</strong>, like G-Floor's ribbed and coin-pattern rolls, is a floor covering rather than a tray. It is thicker, stiffer and nicer looking, and it hides stains, cracks and spalling. It sheds water toward the door through its texture, but it does not contain it. It is the choice for a garage that doubles as a workshop or a gym, or simply for someone who wants the floor to look finished without the prep work and fumes of a coating.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Absorbent mats and drip pads</strong> use a fibrous top over a waterproof backing. They come in two scales: car-length parking mats like the Armor All Original and the G-Floor Drip and Dry, and small pads like the Armor All and Drymate oil spill mats that sit under an engine. They soak up drips rather than letting them stand, which makes them less slippery and better for oil, but they cannot hold a winter's worth of meltwater.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plenty of garages benefit from two kinds at once. A common and effective setup in snow country is a containment mat for winter, rolled away in spring, with a drip pad left under the engine year round. On an epoxy-coated or tiled floor, a drip pad alone is often enough, because the coating already shrugs off water and salt.
          </p>
        </div>

