          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best garage floor mat for snow?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A containment mat with a raised edge is the best garage floor mat for snow, because it holds meltwater and road salt in a tray instead of letting it spread. The AutoFloorGuard AFG7918 suits most cars and small SUVs, and the larger AFG8520 suits full-size trucks. Absorbent mats cannot hold a winter's worth of snowmelt.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do garage containment mats really work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, as long as you size them correctly and empty them. A containment mat keeps salty meltwater off the concrete and in one place, where you can squeegee it out the door. Problems come from mats that are too short for the vehicle, or from owners who let water sit until it overflows the edge.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What do you put under a car to catch oil?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use an absorbent oil drip mat with a waterproof backing, such as the 30 by 59 inch Armor All Premium Oil Spill Mat. It soaks up oil, coolant and brake fluid and stops them reaching the concrete. Place it where the drip actually lands, usually under the front third of the car, and fix the leak as soon as you can.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are roll-out vinyl garage floor mats worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Roll-out vinyl, such as G-Floor, is worth it if you want the floor to look finished without the prep and curing time of a coating. It hides stains and cracks and is easy to clean. It does not contain water, so in snowy climates pair it with a containment mat in winter, or choose a coating or tiles instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you keep a garage floor mat from moving?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Containment mats are heavy enough to stay put once flattened. Roll-out vinyl and absorbent mats can creep under hot tires, so tape the leading edge and corners with double-sided carpet tape, which some mats include. Let a new vinyl mat relax in a warm garage for a day before taping so it lies completely flat.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put a garage mat on an epoxy floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Containment mats and drip pads are safe on cured epoxy and are a common winter addition, since standing brine can dull even a good coating. Wait until the coating is fully cured per the maker's instructions, usually several days, and avoid rubber-backed mats on fresh coatings, which can occasionally discolor them.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you clean a garage containment mat?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Push standing water out with a floor squeegee, sweep up salt residue, then on a mild day roll the mat onto the driveway and wash it with mild detergent and a soft brush. Rinse, let both sides dry completely, and store it rolled rather than folded so the containment edge does not crease.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
